    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i've heard people talk about the vagus nerve being involved in that process too. can you explain how the breathing actually triggers the vagus nerve and how that ties into the parasympathetic response?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The vagus nerve plays a key role in the parasympathetic response associated with box breathing. When you inhale and hold for 4 counts, it stimulates the vagus nerve, particularly the infinitely-connected epithelial cells of the lungs, which helps regulate the parasympathetic nervous system. This stimulation sends signals to the brain, promoting the release of neurotransmitters like acetylcholine, which in turn activates the parasympathetic response, characterized by a decrease in the fight-or-flight response, slower heart rate, and lowered blood pressure.",
